a few use cases and best

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.710
practices about how to
use and implement bots.

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.600
Let's see a few examples.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:24.840
This is one of my favorite bots.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.550
It's called Amy Ingram.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.780
It's by a startup
called X.AI in New York.

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:33.480
And they're actually
an email bot.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:38.180
X.AI developed this
bot and what it does,

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:39.900
it schedules your meetings.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.290
So if you email me and say, hey
Amir, I want to meet next week,

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.810
I add Amy to the email.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.660
It has access to
my calendar and it

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:51.770
starts setting my
emails-- it starts setting

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:53.730
my meetings through email.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.510
It sends about 400
emails a month for me.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.630
So it saves me a lot
of business processes

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.250
that I don't need to do.

00:03:02.250 --> 00:03:05.490
People send thank you
letter for Amy and CC me.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:07.410
They're not aware
that it's software.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:09.540
So it's pretty
awesome and amazing

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:13.740
to have this companion
that helps you do things.

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.800
Another bot that I really
like is the Stats bot.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:20.220
It actually connects to
the Google Analytics API

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.670
and surfaces reports
inside Slack.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.680
So you can actually--
this is my blog.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:28.770
As you can see it's not
performing very well.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.410
But I can actually
talk to the bot

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:34.010
and ask it things like,
where are my users, where

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:35.910
they're coming from,
have a conversation

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.410
about my statistics.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.740
And I don't need to do the
context switching between Slack

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:41.940
and Google Analytics.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:46.610
I get everything in a
conversational interface.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.220
At the technical level,
there's a few differences

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:53.300
between bots and applications
that you know, for example,

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.160
for Android.

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:59.270
And the contrast is that
bots are not binary that

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:02.000
are installed on your client.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.950
We don't install
anything on your client.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.080
We connect services.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.410
So your bot actually lives
on Google Cloud functions,

00:04:09.410 --> 00:04:11.720
or any other hosting
provider, and it

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:15.350
gets event about what's
happening in Slack

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:16.589
for your users.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:19.670
And you can actually interact
with the client using our web

00:04:19.670 --> 00:04:21.620
API.

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:25.010
And then the Slack servers
talk to the Slack client

00:04:25.010 --> 00:04:26.480
using a socket.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.750
So you can actually abstract
and provide a lot of security

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:30.740
using this model.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:33.470
But your code is not installed
on the Slack client--

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:37.090
just want to make that clear.

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.410
So now let's talk a little
bit about bot interactions.

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.710
When people say bots,
they think an entity that

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:44.940
has a conversation with me.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.220
They start thinking NLP, and
it's not necessarily the case.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.100
So we'll go through
a few use cases

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.240
and see where NLP and AI might
make sense and some simple use

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.500
cases that it might not.

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:02.450
So the first type of bot
interactions are notifications.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:06.440
Notifications are an easy way
to pipe content into Slack.

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.500
In this example, we're
using the calendar API

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:11.810
to pipe content
about my meetings.

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.047
Why is this useful?

00:05:13.047 --> 00:05:14.630
Because I don't need
to do the context

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:17.360
switching between my
Google Calendar and Slack.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:19.580
I get my notifications in Slack.

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:23.270
It also improves transparency
and actionability

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:26.150
because, think, if my team
sees this notification,

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:28.730
they know not to ping me.

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:30.710
They know that I'm in a meeting.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:34.160
Think of a message that
is sent into by email

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:38.360
to all of your DevOps versus
a message that goes in Slack.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:41.270
A single message makes
it actionable and more

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.090
transparent about what happened
and what are the actions

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:48.140
that we need to do in order to
resolve an issue, for example.

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:49.820
Does that make sense?

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:51.690
Awesome.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.970
At the technical level,
notifications are very simple.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:57.780
They are a URL that
you expose to you.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.750
You hit that URL with
a certain payload

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.642
and resurface that
information inside Slack.

00:06:03.642 --> 00:06:05.100
So as you can see,
you can actually

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:07.780
use a curl command
to call and create

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:09.720
a notification inside Slack.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.340
It's as easy as a curl command.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.830
The second type of
interaction that we've seen

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:18.600
is slash commands.

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.260
Slash commands are an easy way
to use Slack as the command

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.010
line for a third party service.

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:27.720
In this example, we
work with Foursquare

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:33.300
to surface locations around
for lunch and business.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:37.230
As you can see, you do slash
Foursquare business lunch

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.340
in Miami.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:42.060
We hit the Foursquare servers,
and what they return to us

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:45.840
is what we show in Slack.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.880
So at the technical level, it's
an API that you expose to us.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.310
So the user hits
the slash command,

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.890
we send you-- we
hit your endpoint

00:06:55.890 --> 00:06:57.760
and send you all the
information, like who

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.090
is the user, what's
the team they're on,

00:07:00.090 --> 00:07:03.870
what is the URL-- what is
the query string that they

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.430
added to the slash command.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:11.010
And what you reply to us is
what we display in Slack.

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.830
And this is a simple
example about how to show

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:17.610
"hello" with the username.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.470
The last type of
interaction is actually

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:21.530
a conversational interface.

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:24.360
And this is a full
bot conversation.

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.640
And this is very interesting
because you can actually

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.010
facilitate a lot of interesting
business use cases using bots.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:33.660
In this example,
we work with Howdy

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:35.654
to facilitate stand up meetings.

00:07:35.654 --> 00:07:37.320
Are you familiar with
stand up meetings?

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.230
So we stand up and we say,
what are we working today?

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:41.700
What are we working on tomorrow?

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:42.990
What are we blocked on?

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:44.580
And that happens all the time.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.420
And it's really easy in small
teams that work together,

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:51.360
but it becomes much,
much harder when

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.580
you have big teams that are
distributed around the world.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:58.080
So Howdy actually goes into
each of the team member

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:00.450
around the world in
Slack, and asks them,

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.970
what are you doing today,
what are you doing tomorrow,

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:06.690
what are you blocked on, and
reports that back into Slack.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:09.150
So taking something very
simple and automating it

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.280
through a conversation
actually makes

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.870
a lot of sense in this case.

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:16.620
Building bots is not as
easy as building a slash

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:18.780
command or notification.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:22.530
So we partnered with Howdy
to create an open source Node

00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:25.050
framework for building bots.

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.960
And even if you're not
familiar with Node,

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:31.380
you can see more or less
what the bot is trying to do.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.110
In this example, it's
looking for hello, hi,

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:37.980
or a greeting in a way of
direct mention, mention,

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:40.230
or direct message.

00:08:40.230 --> 00:08:43.140
And what it does, it
replies with Hello.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:46.140
So we actually took the
aspects of conversation

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.860
and made it much easier
using this Node framework.

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:53.980
And I highly recommend
you check BotKit.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.740
On top of that, we
released buttons.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:59.530
And I've never been so
excited about adding buttons

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:03.250
to a feature since we did this.

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:05.650
Buttons are amazing
because they let

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:08.850
users take action inside Slack.

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:11.230
In this example,
Greenhouse-- you

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:14.280
want to approve a
request for a candidate.

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:17.590
You click on the button
and the message changes.

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:20.530
So you can actually create
this illusion of an app--

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.980
or not illusion-- the actual
app that lives inside Slack.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.930
And users can take
action inside Slack.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:31.180
Think how awesome it is to get
vacation approval [? cue ?]

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:35.290
just by clicking on a button,
or doing your expense just

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.855
by clicking on another button.

00:09:37.855 --> 00:09:41.320
And these go over all of
the types of interactions

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:42.370
that we talked about.

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:44.350
You can have notifications
with buttons.

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.270
You can have slash
commands with buttons.

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:50.430
And you can have full fledged
conversation with buttons.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.520
To summarize, we have
three types of interactions

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.220
that we've seen this year.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:59.580
Notifications-- easy way
to pipe content into Slack;

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.040
slash commands-- using
Slack as the command line

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:05.640
for other services to
query and take action;

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:10.830
and then full conversation,
and that facilitates workflows.

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:14.220
And augment all of these
with actionable buttons

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:18.060
so users could take action
inside the conversation

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:20.650
interface.

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:25.480
So let's talk a little bit
about use cases that we've seen.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:28.600
The first use case is the CRM.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:30.850
That was the most
requested use case

00:10:30.850 --> 00:10:33.140
we've seen from our clients--

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:35.860
the most thought
over integration.

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:37.900
And as you can see,
the bot actually

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.720
augments the human conversation.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.880
So you can talk in
the sales channel

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:46.750
and have the bot actually
provide good information

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.100
inside sales, in context.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.880
So you don't need to go
from sales force to Slack.

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:55.820
It actually augments
the conversation.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:58.250
The second type of
interaction that we've seen

00:10:58.250 --> 00:11:00.500
is with task management.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:03.200
So Trello had created
an awesome integration

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.590
that you can actually
assign a time to tasks.

00:11:06.590 --> 00:11:09.990
And you can connect to
everything that you can do--

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:11.600
every day, the
small interactions

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.700
that you have in Trello--

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:15.320
inside Slack.

00:11:15.320 --> 00:11:18.320
But what if you want to
build your own integration?

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.734
What if you have
your own process?

00:11:20.734 --> 00:11:22.400
You don't connect to
third party service

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.680
but you have your own process?

00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:30.890
So in this example, Meetup.com,
has created their own process

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:31.570
of--

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:35.030
in this case for getting
backlog from a conversation.

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:37.820
So we're talking in Slack
about all these cool features

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.000
and ideas.

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:41.120
They've implemented
a slash command

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:43.880
to add ideas to a backlog--

00:11:43.880 --> 00:11:45.680
to add tasks to a backlog.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:48.780
And this is actually
powered by functions.

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:50.330
So you can actually create--

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:53.300
not only use apps and
integrations that come out

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:56.800
of the box, you can easily
create your own workflows

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:01.900
that are specific for your
organization and enterprise.

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:04.615
And that's it.

00:12:04.615 --> 00:12:05.982
Thank you.

00:12:05.982 --> 00:12:10.600
[APPLAUSE]

00:12:10.600 --> 00:12:13.550
ALAN HO: So I just want
a show of hands here.

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:18.680
Who has actually tried
building some sort of bot?

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:19.350
Oh wow.

00:12:19.350 --> 00:12:19.850
OK.

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:20.730
This is great.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:21.750
This is really good.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:22.250
OK.

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:25.340
So we're going to go over
how you can build bots

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:27.830
using Google Cloud Services.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:30.560
And the first thing
I want to talk about

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.690
is two concepts that
I want to give to you.

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:35.240
One is the concept
of serverless,

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.340
and the other is the concept--

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:39.800
and then I want to introduce
some of our machine learning

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:41.100
services.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:43.820
So let's first start
with-- serverless

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:46.220
is a big buzz word, right?

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:48.320
So I want to first start
off with a definition

00:12:48.320 --> 00:12:49.870
of serverless.

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.800
So what is serverless computing?

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:54.470
Serverless computing
eliminates the need

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:57.950
for provisioning and
managing individual servers.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:13:01.720
It's not any more, it's
not any less, right?

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:03.350
But the reason why
this is important

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:06.560
is because like an
inordinate amount

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:09.190
of time for developers
and operations

00:13:09.190 --> 00:13:11.070
is spent on this activity.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:13.820
You know, no
manager or CEO said,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:16.910
thank you for managing my
servers last year, right?

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:20.270
So serverless, even
though it's a buzzword,

00:13:20.270 --> 00:13:23.910
it actually has a lot of
financial implications.

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:26.480
The other thing about serverless
that people don't understand

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:28.640
is that serverless
is fundamentally

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:33.560
your stack re-imagined
and simplified, right?

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:36.290
In this scenario, you
don't care about things

00:13:36.290 --> 00:13:38.030
like load balancers.

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:41.480
You let higher level
services take care of it.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:43.310
Even how you write
your applications

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:44.840
are very different.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:49.730
Code that used to live within
your business application

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:51.230
suddenly moves out.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:53.690
So why don't I just kind of
give you a little overview.

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:59.510
So if you have synchronous
requests or asynchronous

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:03.020
events, they're going through
some sort of PubSub mechanism

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:06.380
and they get picked up and sent
to the function as a service.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:09.920
Synchronous requests are
going through some sort

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:11.780
of API gateway.

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:12.800
Now these are kind of--

00:14:12.800 --> 00:14:15.380
the API gateway is
optional, but the reality

00:14:15.380 --> 00:14:17.640
is that if you build
any production system,

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:21.570
you're probably going to need
the API gateway in front of it.

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:24.720
The function as a service
is where your actual code

00:14:24.720 --> 00:14:26.400
execution occurs.

00:14:26.400 --> 00:14:28.290
And I'm going to use this--

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.080
again, a lot of terms here.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:32.250
A backend as a service
or database as a service

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:34.680
is where your application
store happens.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:37.070
So I want to deep dive into
a function as a service

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:39.510
and backend as a service.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:41.380
So a function as a
service is very simple.

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:45.420
It's basically a place
that you can upload code--

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:46.950
not a virtual machine, right?

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:48.575
You're not uploading
a virtual machine.

00:14:48.575 --> 00:14:50.040
You're just uploading code.

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:51.600
It's event based.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:53.430
There's monitoring built in.

00:14:53.430 --> 00:14:56.564
And you're paying
by every request.

00:14:56.564 --> 00:14:58.980
Now, I want to first talk about
the first part-- uploading

00:14:58.980 --> 00:14:59.790
code.

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:03.630
So uploading code versus
a VM is very important

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:11.580
because it allows the cloud
provider to optimize the system

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:13.570
to run your application.

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:16.290
So today, maybe your
application might

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:19.920
be running on an Intel processor
and tomorrow it might actually

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:22.260
be running on an ARM processor.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:23.790
Those kinds of things only--

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:27.240
that's only possible if you
give the ability for the cloud

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:33.240
provider to optimize your code
for the given compute platform.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:34.380
Now there's some gotchas.

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:39.390
The biggest gotcha is you don't
have access to the file system.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:42.120
You have to make your
application stateless.

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:44.910
And the reason why is
because it enables the cloud

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:47.940
provider to spin up your
function more quickly--

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:50.490
spin up a new instance
quickly, which enables

00:15:50.490 --> 00:15:52.200
things like auto scaling.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.420
But not having access
to the file system

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:57.630
is kind of tricky because
code that used to work--

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:03.240
A.K.A. things such as loading
configuration files from

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:07.080
the file system, things
like disk based caches,

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:11.010
things like installing
your own logging system--

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:13.920
those suddenly don't work
within a function as a service

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:15.070
environment.

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:17.550
So you have to rely on
different mechanisms

00:16:17.550 --> 00:16:21.130
to do things that
you used to do.

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:22.960
The second concept that
I want to introduce

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:25.480
is a backend as a service,
or what you people call

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:27.310
a database as a service.

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:30.700
What differentiates,
let's say, a database

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:32.140
as a service versus--

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:38.140
a truly serverless database
as a service versus something

00:16:38.140 --> 00:16:41.350
like Cassandra or
MongoDB, is that each

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:44.350
of the databases underneath
the hood are multi-tenant.

00:16:44.350 --> 00:16:48.400
These are usually
NoSQL, though if you

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:51.739
looked at our announcement
about Spanner,

00:16:51.739 --> 00:16:53.530
it's one of the first
horizontally scalable

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:57.340
relational database,
that's rapidly changing.

00:16:57.340 --> 00:16:59.410
And there's going to
be logging built in.

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:01.690
And you pay by
request in storage.

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:03.340
There are some gotchas, though.

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:07.000
Usually with these kind
of database as a service

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:10.000
or backend as a service,
you have limited ability

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:13.890
to configure your
indexes, right?

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:16.359
So this is going
to be impacting you

00:17:16.359 --> 00:17:19.390
if you have some extremely
low latency requirements

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:22.619
or you have a very,
very complex query.

00:17:22.619 --> 00:17:24.640
There's times that you
won't be able to use

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:30.730
a backend as a service to
fulfill that capability.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:33.470
We have three
different types of--

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:35.360
for Google, we have
three different types.

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:39.160
We have Cloud Datastore,
we have Firebase,

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:41.140
which is our mobile
backend as a service, which

00:17:41.140 --> 00:17:44.380
actually leverages underneath
the hood Cloud Datastore.

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:47.800
And we also-- if you need to
run a backend as a service

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:51.140
in your own data center, we
also have Apache User Grid.

00:17:51.140 --> 00:17:56.300
And that's being used to run
a lot of enterprises as well.

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:58.380
So let's give you
an example of this

00:17:58.380 --> 00:18:00.100
from a scalability standpoint.

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:04.250
Pokemon, when they first
came to Google and they said,

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:06.460
OK, we're going to
launch this game--

00:18:06.460 --> 00:18:09.310
and it turned out that
after a couple of months

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:13.450
they were doing 50 times the
traffic that they originally

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:14.360
thought.

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:17.397
So these systems are very,
very scalable and very good,

00:18:17.397 --> 00:18:19.480
especially for apps that
you don't know if they're

00:18:19.480 --> 00:18:22.400
going to take off, right?

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:25.150
All right, so recapping the
benefits of serverless-- number

00:18:25.150 --> 00:18:27.850
one, it's much lower
infrastructure costs

00:18:27.850 --> 00:18:31.320
because you're getting
economies of scale.

00:18:31.320 --> 00:18:34.360
You will have a lot
lower operational cost

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:36.730
because a lot of the
routine things like auto

00:18:36.730 --> 00:18:40.070
scaling your application, or
adding more servers, things

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:44.080
of that sort, you're going to
let the cloud provider provide

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:44.867
that.

00:18:44.867 --> 00:18:47.200
But most importantly, I think
there's a part that people

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:49.090
don't think about serverless.

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:53.060
It's a much more simplified
programming model.

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:55.690
That means that even though
your code has limitations,

00:18:55.690 --> 00:18:58.720
it lets you run faster
and execute and build

00:18:58.720 --> 00:18:59.770
your code faster.

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:00.750
You focus on the code.

00:19:03.216 --> 00:19:05.590
So I want to talk a little
bit about the machine learning

00:19:05.590 --> 00:19:06.910
services for building your bot.

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:10.080
So Google has a lot of different
machine learning services you

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:11.500
probably already heard about.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:13.450
And if you think
about this, it's

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:16.750
actually segmented
into two categories--

00:19:16.750 --> 00:19:20.710
machine learning services that
already has the model built in.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:23.310
So you don't actually have
the pre-train a new model.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:26.240
And machine learning services
where you bring your own data,

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:29.440
you bring your own model--
namely cloud machine learning.

00:19:29.440 --> 00:19:32.590
But before I'm going to dive
into which particular service

00:19:32.590 --> 00:19:35.800
you need, the first
question you to ask yourself

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:38.650
is, do you really need AI?

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:41.590
Earlier, you saw that
there were three types

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:45.460
of interactions-- notifications,
slash commands, and then

00:19:45.460 --> 00:19:48.420
full conversational bots.

00:19:48.420 --> 00:19:50.550
The reality is, for
notifications, you

00:19:50.550 --> 00:19:51.880
don't need AI, right?

00:19:51.880 --> 00:19:54.266
I mean, there's an event
trigger that tells--

00:19:54.266 --> 00:19:55.640
it's a one-way
communication that

00:19:55.640 --> 00:19:57.600
tells a person
something has happened.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:19:59.655
You really don't need AI.

00:19:59.655 --> 00:20:04.320
For slash commands, if
it's a very simple command,

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:06.780
you probably don't need AI.

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:10.590
But if you have a command
that is taking in multiple

00:20:10.590 --> 00:20:12.750
parameters-- for
example, in this case,

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:16.320
it says business lunch in Miami,
and your system needs to be

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:18.060
able to react to that--

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:22.590
having AI, namely natural
language processing, helps.

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:25.950
It also helps with dealing with
things like spelling mistakes--

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:27.630
fuzzy matching.

00:20:27.630 --> 00:20:29.400
And then for full on
conversational bots

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:32.160
you definitely need AI, because
people could say anything.

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:35.730
You can't create some massive
if-then-else statement

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:38.780
and expect it to work.

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:40.870
So what does natural
language processing?

00:20:40.870 --> 00:20:41.710
It's very simple.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:45.040
What it does is that it
takes unstructured texts,

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:49.540
unstructured conversations,
and turns them into machine

00:20:49.540 --> 00:20:53.450
understandable user intents.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:58.960
And very aptly named API.AI
is using AI to turn a human

00:20:58.960 --> 00:21:04.070
conversation into an application
programming understandable--

00:21:04.070 --> 00:21:04.770
sorry--

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:07.080
something-- turning
it into an API.

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:10.360
So that's a very, very good name
for that particular product.

00:21:13.520 --> 00:21:15.210
So when you pull
it all together,

00:21:15.210 --> 00:21:17.230
you pull the
architecture together,

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:19.967
you have your clients on
the very left-hand side.

00:21:19.967 --> 00:21:21.550
They're going to be
talking, probably,

00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:25.870
to some API Management,
API gateway that

00:21:25.870 --> 00:21:32.200
takes care of things like
security and authorization,

00:21:32.200 --> 00:21:33.760
things of those sort.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:37.800
Your application code lives
in your cloud functions.

00:21:37.800 --> 00:21:41.800
Your application data lives in
something like Cloud Datastore.

00:21:41.800 --> 00:21:45.850
And the business logic would
talk to the Machine Learning

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:49.120
Services to interpret
what the user is saying

00:21:49.120 --> 00:21:51.400
and give back user intents.

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:55.450
So we're going to have
a quick demo here.

00:21:55.450 --> 00:21:58.000
And for this demo, Amir
is going to help me.

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:05.110
I decided that I want to build
a bot that kind of solves

00:22:05.110 --> 00:22:06.430
my problem.

00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:09.640
And one of the things that
I really hate about bots--

00:22:09.640 --> 00:22:12.960
I mean, sorry-- about
conversations via text

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:15.910
is sometimes when you're
in chat you don't quite

00:22:15.910 --> 00:22:17.550
get a lot of emotion in chat.

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:18.250
It's not fun.

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:20.170
Like you do your daily work.

00:22:20.170 --> 00:22:21.220
It's not fun.

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:25.640
Sometimes people like me, we're
not that great with emojis.

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:28.570
So you sacrifice a
little bit of fun.

00:22:28.570 --> 00:22:31.840
The second thing that really
hurts me when I'm in chat

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:36.960
is when I'm on a deadline,
something I say to my teammate

00:22:36.960 --> 00:22:40.090
may come off as really
harsh criticism.

00:22:40.090 --> 00:22:42.880
So for example, like I need to--

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:44.640
if I'm getting a deadline--

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:46.130
meeting a deadline,
I just say, you

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:47.990
missed the deadline,
something of that sort.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:49.930
I often say things
are extremely curt

00:22:49.930 --> 00:22:52.540
and it can be
misinterpreted criticism.

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:56.590
So can I create a bot that
makes these conversations

00:22:56.590 --> 00:22:57.880
a little bit more fun?

00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:00.370
Can I create a bot
that would tell me

00:23:00.370 --> 00:23:02.860
if I've said some boneheaded
thing that comes off

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:05.240
as criticism.

00:23:05.240 --> 00:23:07.920
So I decided to create
something called KeanuBot.

00:23:07.920 --> 00:23:10.960
And what KeanuBot
does is that he

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:14.740
looks at all the conversations
and tries to figure out

00:23:14.740 --> 00:23:17.050
a phrase from a movie--

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:20.500
you know, classics,
like "Point Break"--

00:23:20.500 --> 00:23:23.920
that would either
liven the conversation

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:29.880
or be able to take the sting
out of bad, curt remarks.

00:23:29.880 --> 00:23:33.290
So I'm going to-- actually
can you switch to the demo

00:23:33.290 --> 00:23:35.550
and Amir will demo with me?

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:38.470
All right.

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:40.136
So I might say something
like, hi, Amir.

00:23:43.375 --> 00:23:44.166
AMIR SHEVAT: Hello.

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:51.940
ALAN HO: How are things going?

00:23:51.940 --> 00:23:55.621
AMIR SHEVAT: I'm going to
answer my typical answer--

00:23:55.621 --> 00:23:56.120
amazing.

00:24:00.366 --> 00:24:01.800
Whoa.

00:24:01.800 --> 00:24:09.850
Or I might say,
my car broke down.

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:13.420
Oh, bogus.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:18.500
ALAN HO: All right, so let's
take another example, like Amir

00:24:18.500 --> 00:24:22.370
and I were interacting
on some dot--

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:29.200
on this next presentation and
I'm like, oh my gosh, Amir,

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:39.425
you missed the deadline for
submitting your draft to legal.

00:24:46.310 --> 00:24:48.180
Yeah, KeanuBot is
just reminding me

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:52.090
that, OK, maybe that's
a little bit too harsh.

00:24:52.090 --> 00:24:54.810
I got to tone
things down, right?

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:55.440
OK.

00:24:55.440 --> 00:24:56.967
So let's go back to the slides.

00:24:56.967 --> 00:24:58.800
I want to actually show
you the architecture

00:24:58.800 --> 00:25:00.900
and how that works.

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:03.757
So what I just showed
you is the Slack--

00:25:03.757 --> 00:25:05.340
I want to go through
the Slack client,

00:25:05.340 --> 00:25:10.210
talking to Slack server,
talking to an API gateway--

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:12.810
which is Apigee's API gateway--

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:16.380
talking to cloud functions,
talking to API.AI

00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:18.010
and giving back response.

00:25:18.010 --> 00:25:18.720
All right.

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:20.180
So let's go back to the demo.

00:25:23.920 --> 00:25:27.520
So I want to show you--
within Slack there's a UI

00:25:27.520 --> 00:25:29.250
that you can use
to create a bot.

00:25:29.250 --> 00:25:31.360
And within the
bot, what you do is

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:34.270
that there is a section
called Event Subscriptions.

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:37.930
So I am going to set the Event
Subscriptions to send events

00:25:37.930 --> 00:25:40.420
to this Apigee gateway.

00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:44.050
And within this
UI I can also set

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:46.210
which events to subscribe to.

00:25:46.210 --> 00:25:49.366
So events might be like
posting new messages,

00:25:49.366 --> 00:25:50.740
but it could also
be other things

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:55.200
like a new user joins
the room, or a new user

00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:57.100
got kicked out of a room.

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:59.290
So your bots can react on
different ways to that.

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:01.480
So in this kind of
scenario, I basically

00:26:01.480 --> 00:26:05.830
subscribed to all the
messages going to the channel.

00:26:05.830 --> 00:26:09.440
The next part I want to
show you is the API proxy--

00:26:09.440 --> 00:26:10.870
sorry-- the API gateway.

00:26:10.870 --> 00:26:14.800
And in the API gateway you
can create one or more APIs

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:15.970
proxies.

00:26:15.970 --> 00:26:19.600
In this scenario, I created
this KeanuBot event handler.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:22.808
And if I go into it--

00:26:22.808 --> 00:26:25.640
oh.

00:26:25.640 --> 00:26:26.820
Let's redeploy.

00:26:39.010 --> 00:26:43.530
Nothing like trying
things out in production.

00:26:43.530 --> 00:26:48.170
So what you have here is that
it shows the URLs over here

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:51.090
and it points it to a
particular endpoint target.

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:57.910
What also you can do too--

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:00.530
and I hope that this is
not going to break on me.

00:27:03.390 --> 00:27:04.450
Give me one second here.

00:27:11.210 --> 00:27:13.425
Oh, actually, I'm not going
to play around with that.

00:27:13.425 --> 00:27:15.050
So what I'm going to
actually try doing

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:19.200
is I'm going to try
deploying a filter.

00:27:19.200 --> 00:27:23.120
So also, what the API
management system does

00:27:23.120 --> 00:27:25.910
is that you could do things
like spike arrest filters, which

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:29.270
prevents your bot from getting
flooded with too much traffic,

00:27:29.270 --> 00:27:32.780
or you can apply things
like security, like OAuth2.

00:27:32.780 --> 00:27:36.950
So this is kind of the example
of application security

00:27:36.950 --> 00:27:38.450
code that may have
lived in your app

00:27:38.450 --> 00:27:40.910
before, it's being
moved out of your app

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:44.020
into higher level services.

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:47.460
Let's put a spike arrest
filter on here and let's

00:27:47.460 --> 00:27:57.360
set it to 5, OK, 10
messages per minute.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:27:57.860
OK.

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:01.660
All right.

00:28:01.660 --> 00:28:02.630
So it's submitting it.

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:06.690
While that's
happening, I'm going

00:28:06.690 --> 00:28:08.812
to show you cloud functions.

00:28:08.812 --> 00:28:10.520
So cloud functions--
this is the console.

00:28:10.520 --> 00:28:12.810
Any cloud functions you
deploy will show up here.

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:14.970
You could literally have
hundreds of functions.

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:17.250
That's the great
thing about this.

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:19.830
And you're not going to be
charged for the functions that

00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:21.370
are not running.

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:24.330
It will be spun up on demand.

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:26.700
We could take a
look at the logs.

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:29.080
All the cloud
functions, by default,

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:31.530
have a logger built in.

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:34.620
And you can see here,
the last message

00:28:34.620 --> 00:28:35.720
that was coming through--

00:28:41.300 --> 00:28:43.380
how are things going,
things of that sort,

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:45.865
they all show up
in the log message.

00:28:45.865 --> 00:28:48.115
Let's just double check to
make sure this stuff works.

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:52.790
OK.

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:53.290
All right.

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:56.740
Let's see-- let's
hope it still works.

00:28:56.740 --> 00:29:00.010
I'm going to actually
introduce a bug in the system

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:03.270
and show you how
a deployment goes.

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:05.147
So inside this cloud function--

00:29:05.147 --> 00:29:06.980
to write a cloud function,
it's very simple.

00:29:06.980 --> 00:29:09.680
You basically write
a function over here.

00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:12.260
It looks exactly
like an Express app

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:15.800
because underneath the hood it's
actually leveraging Express.

00:29:15.800 --> 00:29:20.220
And then you basically export
that particular function.

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:24.370
Now, I'm going to put a little
code in here that actually

00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:32.890
causes KeanuBot to ignore all
the requests that are coming in

00:29:32.890 --> 00:29:36.100
from itself, because
KeanuBot is only supposed

00:29:36.100 --> 00:29:38.200
to monitor human responses.

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:42.690
And I'm going to introduce
this bug over here.

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:44.762
And then I'm going to
deploy it to just show you

00:29:44.762 --> 00:29:45.720
how a deployment works.

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:55.620
And it typically takes
under two minutes

00:29:55.620 --> 00:29:58.246
to actually get a
deployment happening.

00:29:58.246 --> 00:29:59.790
So while that's
happening, I want

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:03.380
to quickly show you what's
going to happen with this bug.

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:07.860
So this bug leverages--
is going to cause the bot

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:09.500
to go in an infinite loop.

00:30:09.500 --> 00:30:11.210
And the reason why
is because this--

00:30:11.210 --> 00:30:17.030
I created in API.AI
a set of intents.

00:30:17.030 --> 00:30:19.010
And one of intent
is this whoa intent.

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:21.020
So this whoa intent,
basically what it says

00:30:21.020 --> 00:30:24.770
is that if it ever sees
a message called whoa,

00:30:24.770 --> 00:30:28.040
it would respond back
with whoa as well

00:30:28.040 --> 00:30:30.380
with a picture of Keanu Reeves.

00:30:30.380 --> 00:30:33.110
So let's try this out and
let's see if it works.

00:30:36.719 --> 00:30:39.409
Start tracing the session.

00:30:39.409 --> 00:30:41.700
And let's just double check
to make sure it's deployed.

00:30:41.700 --> 00:30:43.660
OK.

00:30:43.660 --> 00:30:44.200
And then--

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:50.110
All right.

00:30:50.110 --> 00:30:52.360
So now it's going
into its infinite--

00:30:52.360 --> 00:30:54.482
it went into an infinite loop.

00:30:54.482 --> 00:30:56.190
So let's go take a
look at what happened.

00:30:56.190 --> 00:30:58.405
So one of the cool things--

00:30:58.405 --> 00:31:03.300
oh, give me one second here.

00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:11.070
Again.

00:31:14.850 --> 00:31:19.580
Well, I'm having some issues
with the UI for the API

00:31:19.580 --> 00:31:21.240
management system.

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:23.300
But what's happening
here is, actually,

00:31:23.300 --> 00:31:26.740
if you look in the
logs, you can see

00:31:26.740 --> 00:31:30.730
that it was sending whoa,
whoa, multiple times.

00:31:30.730 --> 00:31:33.970
And then eventually it stopped,
because what was happening

00:31:33.970 --> 00:31:38.080
was at the API gateway, we
had set a spike arrest filter

00:31:38.080 --> 00:31:41.290
and it basically was able to
block that flood of messages

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:42.472
coming in.

00:31:42.472 --> 00:31:43.930
So before that I'm
going to-- let's

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:45.400
go fix the code and redeploy.

00:31:57.334 --> 00:31:58.750
And while that's
happening, I want

00:31:58.750 --> 00:32:02.240
to show you how you can
use API.AI to create

00:32:02.240 --> 00:32:03.850
a new intent on the fly.

00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:06.080
So I'm going to
create an intent.

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:08.230
Inside Slack channel,
a lot of times people

00:32:08.230 --> 00:32:12.040
ask like, how do you do this,
or I don't know how to do that.

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:18.470
So I might say something
like does anyone

00:32:18.470 --> 00:32:21.160
know how to do something?

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:22.460
Right?

00:32:22.460 --> 00:32:24.530
And whenever
somebody says this, I

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:28.670
think about the Keanu
Reeves quote in "Matrix"

00:32:28.670 --> 00:32:31.010
where he says, oh
I know Kung Fu.

00:32:31.010 --> 00:32:34.320
So we will take this
picture and we can put it

00:32:34.320 --> 00:32:35.195
in the text response.

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:43.370
And we'll save this--

00:32:43.370 --> 00:32:45.140
and we'll save it here.

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:47.150
And once we create
the intent, we

00:32:47.150 --> 00:32:49.820
can actually try it out
within the API.AI UI.

00:32:49.820 --> 00:32:52.130
So it doesn't have to
be exactly the same.

00:32:52.130 --> 00:32:55.490
I'll say something
like, Amir, do

00:32:55.490 --> 00:33:01.370
you know how to
fix my Slack code--

00:33:04.740 --> 00:33:05.900
actually, does anyone?

00:33:11.564 --> 00:33:12.370
Oh, OK.

00:33:12.370 --> 00:33:15.140
So it did get-- it actually
matched a different one.

00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:17.230
Let's try something else.

00:33:17.230 --> 00:33:23.315
Does any one know
how to fix Slack?

00:33:29.554 --> 00:33:30.054
OK.

00:33:37.026 --> 00:33:38.080
Oh, you know what?

00:33:38.080 --> 00:33:39.210
I forgot to press Save.

00:33:39.210 --> 00:33:41.685
[LAUGHS]

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:50.891
Oh, what's going on here?

00:33:56.670 --> 00:34:00.770
OK, it just took a little bit
of time to actually get through.

00:34:00.770 --> 00:34:03.070
So you can actually
test within here.

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:05.140
And what you see
here is that it's

00:34:05.140 --> 00:34:08.100
able to match the
particular intent.

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:10.030
And because it's
not an exact match,

00:34:10.030 --> 00:34:12.040
it actually gives you a score.

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:14.620
So this gives you a
confidence of how confident

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:18.969
your application actually
matched that particular phrase.

00:34:18.969 --> 00:34:25.239
And within your code, you could
basically put a match filter.

00:34:25.239 --> 00:34:27.610
And I said if it matches
more-- if the score is

00:34:27.610 --> 00:34:30.543
higher than 0.5, then it
would actually return.

00:34:30.543 --> 00:34:31.959
So if we actually
go to the code--

00:34:35.620 --> 00:34:43.994
say, does any one know
how to debug Visual Basic?

00:34:48.679 --> 00:34:51.106
And it pops up.

00:34:51.106 --> 00:34:51.980
AMIR SHEVAT: Awesome.

00:34:51.980 --> 00:34:54.690
ALAN HO: So Amir, what do
you think about this bot

00:34:54.690 --> 00:34:55.600
that I created?

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:58.130
And just give me some
comments and feedback on it.

00:34:58.130 --> 00:34:59.671
AMIR SHEVAT: So
first of all, I think

00:34:59.671 --> 00:35:01.630
it's awesome because it
augments conversation.

00:35:01.630 --> 00:35:03.686
So it actually
addresses your problem,

00:35:03.686 --> 00:35:05.310
which is, like,
sometimes we say things

00:35:05.310 --> 00:35:08.130
in text that you don't know how
to interpret with other people,

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:08.630
right?

00:35:08.630 --> 00:35:12.690
And this adds a layer
of this awesomeness.

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:14.610
What I would suggest,
moving forward,

00:35:14.610 --> 00:35:18.570
is maybe having the bot
answer these awesome replies

00:35:18.570 --> 00:35:19.960
in threads.

00:35:19.960 --> 00:35:21.960
So you can actually have
the conversation going,

00:35:21.960 --> 00:35:24.930
but actually have the
bot respond in threads

00:35:24.930 --> 00:35:27.570
so that it doesn't
disturb our conversation.

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:29.220
We can have our
conversation in line

00:35:29.220 --> 00:35:31.560
and if we want to have this
interpretation of the bot

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:33.810
and what it does say
about what we are saying,

00:35:33.810 --> 00:35:36.841
we can click on the threads
and see what the bot is saying.

00:35:36.841 --> 00:35:37.590
Do you understand?

00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:40.920
So that could be a nice
addition to your bot.

00:35:40.920 --> 00:35:42.480
ALAN HO: And you
know, I just showed

00:35:42.480 --> 00:35:44.970
there was a lot of little
errors here and there.

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:47.070
What are some practices
that bot builders

00:35:47.070 --> 00:35:49.700
deal with if there's
a misinterpretation

00:35:49.700 --> 00:35:52.330
or what are some
strategies around that?

00:35:52.330 --> 00:35:53.871
AMIR SHEVAT: That's
a great question.

00:35:53.871 --> 00:35:56.490
I think one of the key
things is read your logs.

00:35:56.490 --> 00:36:01.290
You will see that users say and
interact with your bot in ways

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:03.270
that you didn't think are--

00:36:03.270 --> 00:36:06.150
wasn't the use case
that you thought.

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:09.900
People ask bots about Trump.

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:12.300
They ask thoughts
about the weather.

00:36:12.300 --> 00:36:14.490
They ask Trump about anything.

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:18.090
I even found two
founders that actually

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:20.460
mapped the amount
of times people

00:36:20.460 --> 00:36:23.021
said I love you to the bots.

00:36:23.021 --> 00:36:25.020
Think of it, because this
is an interaction that

00:36:25.020 --> 00:36:29.220
is very human and very
intimate, people really

00:36:29.220 --> 00:36:33.060
tend to have emotion
responses to the bot.

00:36:33.060 --> 00:36:34.920
So don't be surprised
if your bot actually

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:37.740
delivers on what it does.

00:36:37.740 --> 00:36:39.840
It will get a lot of
love from the users.

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:40.760
ALAN HO: OK.

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:41.260
All right.

00:36:41.260 --> 00:36:43.950
So let's go back to slides.

00:36:43.950 --> 00:36:46.362
We're almost at our end.

00:36:46.362 --> 00:36:48.570
So I'd just like to remind
you that if you liked this

00:36:48.570 --> 00:36:51.050
presentation, or
you didn't like it,

00:36:51.050 --> 00:36:56.270
we have two more
presentations-- one on API.AI,

00:36:56.270 --> 00:36:59.850
and then one on how-- instead
of building a Slack bot,

00:36:59.850 --> 00:37:02.310
you're going to be able to
build a-- or a chat bot--

00:37:02.310 --> 00:37:04.380
how to build a voice bot.

00:37:04.380 --> 00:37:06.450
So with that, let's
have some questions.

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:09.366
[NO AUDIO]

00:37:18.620 --> 00:37:22.650
So the question was, why did I
choose Cloud Functions instead

00:37:22.650 --> 00:37:25.560
of App Engine, or Endpoints?

00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:28.290
So the reason why I chose
Cloud Functions is because it

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:31.772
was just so darn easy to do.

00:37:31.772 --> 00:37:34.530
And the truth of the
matter is App Engine--

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:36.510
actually, the code
that I just showed you,

00:37:36.510 --> 00:37:40.070
that same code runs on App
Engine directly as well.

00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:42.300
Some of the nice things
is that if you run it

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:45.600
on Cloud Functions,
you don't have

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:48.690
to pay for when the
app is not running.

00:37:48.690 --> 00:37:51.066
And also it scales very,
very quickly as well.

00:37:51.066 --> 00:37:52.440
Underneath the
hood, they've done

00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:55.650
some optimizations to make
it scale, and it's cheap.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:38:01.920
Endpoints, actually, is what we
call-- is also an API gateway.

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:06.600
And I would actually say that
if you have enterprise use

00:38:06.600 --> 00:38:09.780
cases, especially if you have
multi-cloud enterprise use

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:12.620
cases, Apigee is the way to go.

00:38:12.620 --> 00:38:16.560
And Apigee also provides other
API management capabilities

00:38:16.560 --> 00:38:20.190
like creating developer
portals and more complex ways

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:23.130
of doing--

00:38:23.130 --> 00:38:26.790
it has more complex
policies as well.

00:38:26.790 --> 00:38:29.150
Endpoints, on the other
hand, it's cheaper.

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:30.960
I'll be honest will
you right here.

00:38:30.960 --> 00:38:34.560
But it's also designed for
Google's infrastructure.

00:38:34.560 --> 00:38:38.040
So if you have an app and your
APIs are all within Google

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:42.360
itself, API Endpoints
is a-- sorry--

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:43.860
Cloud Endpoints is
a great solution.

00:38:47.059 --> 00:38:47.850
AUDIENCE: Hi there.

00:38:47.850 --> 00:38:51.520
We tried to build a
small bot in our company

00:38:51.520 --> 00:38:56.250
and one of the issues that we
faced was names recognition.

00:38:56.250 --> 00:39:00.690
In particular, we wanted it
to-- and with the API.AI--

00:39:00.690 --> 00:39:04.110
we wanted it to
recognize people's names.

00:39:04.110 --> 00:39:07.500
And the issue is that
names can be foreign,

00:39:07.500 --> 00:39:11.070
they can be objects that
already exist in the system.

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:13.530
How are you-- are there
any strategies for actually

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:16.260
distinguishing names?

00:39:16.260 --> 00:39:20.010
Do you aggregate everything
from the corporate directory

00:39:20.010 --> 00:39:21.570
and stick that in as a name?

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:23.130
I mean, what are
some things that you

00:39:23.130 --> 00:39:24.540
can do to help solve that?

00:39:24.540 --> 00:39:27.070
AMIR SHEVAT: So there's a few
strategies to handle that.

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:28.920
We have sign-in with Slack.

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:30.690
So when a user
installed the app,

00:39:30.690 --> 00:39:35.370
it can actually get his
email, his team, his name,

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:36.690
or her name.

00:39:36.690 --> 00:39:41.010
You could also use our API
to list the users in the team

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:43.750
and get all the information--
all their profile information

00:39:43.750 --> 00:39:44.250
on them.

00:39:44.250 --> 00:39:45.041
AUDIENCE: Oh sorry.

00:39:45.041 --> 00:39:46.280
I'm sorry I didn't clarify--

00:39:46.280 --> 00:39:47.270
voice-based.

00:39:47.270 --> 00:39:48.047
Voice-based.

00:39:48.047 --> 00:39:49.630
ALAN HO: Oh,
voiced-based application.

00:39:49.630 --> 00:39:50.796
AUDIENCE: Yeah, voice-based.

00:39:50.796 --> 00:39:52.767
AMIR SHEVAT: That's
a good question.

00:39:52.767 --> 00:39:54.100
ALAN HO: That's a hard question.

00:39:54.100 --> 00:39:58.720
So if we could actually
go back to the demo.

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:01.160
So this might not clarify
everything that you said,

00:40:01.160 --> 00:40:10.240
but when you create an
intent, say a name intent--

00:40:10.240 --> 00:40:13.789
my name is Alan.

00:40:13.789 --> 00:40:15.330
What it does is that
it automatically

00:40:15.330 --> 00:40:17.480
recognize the name.

00:40:17.480 --> 00:40:20.640
Now, what you can do is you
can create multiple variations

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:24.000
of this and basically--

00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:26.710
Alan is my name.

00:40:26.710 --> 00:40:29.230
What ends up happening
is that it gets smarter.

00:40:29.230 --> 00:40:31.720
It gets smarter by having
multiple of these phrases

00:40:31.720 --> 00:40:32.530
in there.

00:40:32.530 --> 00:40:34.780
So you just basically
have to figure out

00:40:34.780 --> 00:40:39.190
the corpus of phrases that
may have a name in there.

00:40:39.190 --> 00:40:42.160
And you can add them
in and then that way it

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:44.890
will be able to better
pull out the name.

00:40:44.890 --> 00:40:46.640
I know that doesn't
work all the time.

00:40:46.640 --> 00:40:48.655
But that's maybe one
strategy you can use.

00:40:48.655 --> 00:40:48.910
AUDIENCE: All right.

00:40:48.910 --> 00:40:49.410
Thanks.

00:40:53.490 --> 00:40:54.923
ALAN HO: Any other questions?

00:40:54.923 --> 00:40:57.821
[NO AUDIO]

00:41:09.230 --> 00:41:12.590
AMIR SHEVAT: So the question
is, are engineers biased?

00:41:12.590 --> 00:41:14.510
Are we using
engineering speech when

00:41:14.510 --> 00:41:16.130
we're doing speech recognition?

00:41:16.130 --> 00:41:18.590
And the answer is yes.

00:41:18.590 --> 00:41:20.180
I'm an engineer so I can say it.

00:41:20.180 --> 00:41:22.850
But I think us, as engineer,
we have natural language

00:41:22.850 --> 00:41:24.370
understanding problems.

00:41:24.370 --> 00:41:25.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:26.240
Right?

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:27.220
It's true.

00:41:27.220 --> 00:41:28.910
It's something that we have.

00:41:28.910 --> 00:41:32.840
So I think having someone
who's an actual designer,

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:35.240
a conversational designer,
is very important.

00:41:35.240 --> 00:41:39.050
Having a scriptwriter-- so,
for example, Hi Poncho--

00:41:39.050 --> 00:41:43.070
which is a bot that
helps you do the weather,

00:41:43.070 --> 00:41:45.290
they actually hire
people who are

00:41:45.290 --> 00:41:47.030
doing conversational designs.

00:41:47.030 --> 00:41:50.090
They have scripters who
actually-- and they have a CMS.

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:51.740
They have content
management system

00:41:51.740 --> 00:41:56.180
that actually maps conversation
and what the bot will say.

00:41:56.180 --> 00:41:57.140
So yeah.

00:41:57.140 --> 00:41:59.450
Let's not have engineers
create the scripts.

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:02.264
Let's have designers
create the scripts.

00:42:02.264 --> 00:42:04.699
[NO AUDIO]

00:42:12.510 --> 00:42:13.240
AMIR SHEVAT: OK.

00:42:13.240 --> 00:42:13.740
Yeah.

00:42:13.740 --> 00:42:15.190
ALAN HO: Can you
repeat the question?

00:42:15.190 --> 00:42:17.023
AMIR SHEVAT: So the
question was, how do you

00:42:17.023 --> 00:42:18.070
set the expectations?

00:42:18.070 --> 00:42:21.010
What can the bot do or not do?

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:24.940
I think the key, in Slack,
is around onboarding.

00:42:24.940 --> 00:42:28.060
So when you onboard
an app or a bot,

00:42:28.060 --> 00:42:31.510
the key is to let the team
know what the bot could do.

00:42:31.510 --> 00:42:34.490
Think of-- and it's the
same thing with humans.

00:42:34.490 --> 00:42:38.830
So when you onboard legal aid
to the team, what do you say?

00:42:38.830 --> 00:42:42.220
You say, hey everybody, we
have this new legal aid.

00:42:42.220 --> 00:42:45.400
They could help you
with legal questions.

00:42:45.400 --> 00:42:47.410
And you can use
the legal channel

00:42:47.410 --> 00:42:50.680
to communicate with her or him.

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:52.150
It's the same thing with bots.

00:42:52.150 --> 00:42:55.090
So you can actually script
an onboarding script

00:42:55.090 --> 00:43:00.340
that lets the team know how to
use the bot and what to expect.

00:43:00.340 --> 00:43:05.620
The second thing is to
handle errors and feedback.

00:43:05.620 --> 00:43:08.125
So there's a lot of things that
people will say to the bot.

00:43:08.125 --> 00:43:10.000
And they will ask it,
they will play with it.

00:43:10.000 --> 00:43:14.680
Because this is a new user
interface, people like to play.

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:16.960
So just manage expectations.

00:43:16.960 --> 00:43:20.410
Don't try to do, for example,
don't develop chit chat model.

00:43:20.410 --> 00:43:23.440
A lot of developers, which
I've met, try to like-- hello.

00:43:23.440 --> 00:43:23.970
How are you?

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:24.450
What's the weather?

00:43:24.450 --> 00:43:25.570
How have you been doing?

00:43:25.570 --> 00:43:27.760
That's a total waste of time.

00:43:27.760 --> 00:43:30.760
Be very, very clear
with what the bot does

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:32.860
and have a single
purpose bot that

00:43:32.860 --> 00:43:37.090
solves one thing and people
will hopefully love it.

00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:39.190
ALAN HO: Another suggestion
is that, especially

00:43:39.190 --> 00:43:42.850
if you're trying to deploy
into enterprise, don't always--

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:44.890
you might have a
50-50 model where

00:43:44.890 --> 00:43:46.900
the bot answers 50%
of the questions,

00:43:46.900 --> 00:43:49.180
and a human answers
the other 50%.

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:52.990
So an example, another use
case on the Apigee website

00:43:52.990 --> 00:43:57.850
itself, we created a bot that
would majority of the time

00:43:57.850 --> 00:44:01.260
actually be answered by
inside salesperson, right?

00:44:01.260 --> 00:44:03.370
Like if somebody's asking
for a pricing sheet

00:44:03.370 --> 00:44:05.177
or needs to sign up for a demo.

00:44:05.177 --> 00:44:06.760
But for the things
that are very, very

00:44:06.760 --> 00:44:11.960
common, like how do I implement
OAuth, the bot would actually--

00:44:11.960 --> 00:44:16.315
the application would actually
intercept that message

00:44:16.315 --> 00:44:18.790
that's going to the
inside salesperson,

00:44:18.790 --> 00:44:21.580
reply back immediately
to that person

00:44:21.580 --> 00:44:23.980
with an answer that's suitable.

00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:27.100
And to the user, it looks
like the inside salesperson

00:44:27.100 --> 00:44:28.930
is actually making the answer.

00:44:28.930 --> 00:44:31.480
So that way you can
actually, kind of--

00:44:31.480 --> 00:44:35.200
you can create-- you can
intercept messages at a very--

00:44:35.200 --> 00:44:40.480
in that scenario, you want to
make your AI system intercept

00:44:40.480 --> 00:44:43.420
very, very specific
messages and fallback

00:44:43.420 --> 00:44:45.370
to the human majority
of the times,

00:44:45.370 --> 00:44:48.310
because a lot of times we
just haven't planned for it.

00:44:48.310 --> 00:44:51.190
And that way you can,
over time, gradually

00:44:51.190 --> 00:44:56.119
train up the bot to add more
and more new functionality.

00:44:56.119 --> 00:44:57.410
AMIR SHEVAT: I agree with that.

00:44:57.410 --> 00:45:00.020
Maybe last comment on
that is that not all bots

00:45:00.020 --> 00:45:00.980
are conversational.

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:02.670
Again, this is very important.

00:45:02.670 --> 00:45:06.620
Most of the bots I've seen
are notifications and business

00:45:06.620 --> 00:45:07.640
workflows.

00:45:07.640 --> 00:45:09.920
These do not require
a lot of conversation.

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:13.440
It's just getting
good things done.

00:45:13.440 --> 00:45:15.500
So it's a matter of,
like, you can facilitate

00:45:15.500 --> 00:45:18.170
a lot with buttons
and rich interaction

00:45:18.170 --> 00:45:20.780
and then the process
becomes much more

00:45:20.780 --> 00:45:23.621
intuitive for your users.

00:45:23.621 --> 00:45:25.609
[NO AUDIO]

00:46:00.100 --> 00:46:01.350
AMIR SHEVAT: Yeah, definitely.

00:46:01.350 --> 00:46:04.730
So this is called DM,
Direct Message, and a bot

00:46:04.730 --> 00:46:07.410
could DM any user.

00:46:07.410 --> 00:46:09.470
So what I would see
is like you start

00:46:09.470 --> 00:46:12.950
doing these sentences
inside the channel

00:46:12.950 --> 00:46:14.370
and the bot analyzes this.

00:46:14.370 --> 00:46:16.610
This would actually-- we
should implement that idea

00:46:16.610 --> 00:46:17.750
for the KeanuBot, right?

00:46:17.750 --> 00:46:19.792
And then the KeanuBot
could give you in a DM

00:46:19.792 --> 00:46:21.750
like an interpretation
of what you said, right?

00:46:21.750 --> 00:46:24.272
You did-- this was
too harsh, right?

00:46:24.272 --> 00:46:25.730
So you can actually
implement a way

00:46:25.730 --> 00:46:28.490
where the bot listens
to the channel

00:46:28.490 --> 00:46:31.052
but gives you feedback
in a Direct Message.

00:46:31.052 --> 00:46:32.410
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:32.410 --> 00:46:33.400
AMIR SHEVAT: No.

00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:34.560
No, no, no, no.

00:46:34.560 --> 00:46:35.470
Yeah.

00:46:35.470 --> 00:46:36.210
Sorry.

00:46:36.210 --> 00:46:37.966
Yeah and sorry.

00:46:37.966 --> 00:46:38.958
[LAUGHS]

00:46:38.958 --> 00:46:40.446
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:46:46.382 --> 00:46:47.090
AMIR SHEVAT: Wow.

00:46:47.090 --> 00:46:48.881
ALAN HO: Why don't you
repeat the question.

00:46:48.881 --> 00:46:51.070
AMIR SHEVAT: I love all my bots.

00:46:51.070 --> 00:46:54.490
I love bots that facilitate
business processes.

00:46:54.490 --> 00:46:56.965
So for example, I ask
this in every event

00:46:56.965 --> 00:46:58.450
so I'm going to ask this here.

00:46:58.450 --> 00:47:00.365
How many of you like
to do expense reports?

00:47:03.140 --> 00:47:05.270
Not a lot of people
raised their hands.

00:47:05.270 --> 00:47:06.410
And why is that?

00:47:06.410 --> 00:47:09.020
Because if you think about
doing expense reports,

00:47:09.020 --> 00:47:10.580
it's actually an
awesome-- should

00:47:10.580 --> 00:47:12.200
be an awesome experience.

00:47:12.200 --> 00:47:14.410
I paid for something
and now the company

00:47:14.410 --> 00:47:16.560
is paying me back, right?

00:47:16.560 --> 00:47:20.610
We should wake up every morning
wanting to do expense reports.

00:47:20.610 --> 00:47:24.560
But if you ask VPs, they don't
mind doing expense reports.

00:47:24.560 --> 00:47:25.060
Why?

00:47:25.060 --> 00:47:27.950
I paid for something and
now my personal assistant

00:47:27.950 --> 00:47:32.270
helps me and makes that pain
go away and then I get paid.

00:47:32.270 --> 00:47:36.320
So think of all the business
processes that are suck-y--

00:47:36.320 --> 00:47:40.790
for lack of any other
name-- in your business life

00:47:40.790 --> 00:47:44.540
and see how you can implement
them using simple workflows.

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:48.050
Most of our business processes
are in spreadsheets or VB

00:47:48.050 --> 00:47:48.740
script--

00:47:48.740 --> 00:47:50.300
God forbid.

00:47:50.300 --> 00:47:52.430
You can actually take a
lot of these processes

00:47:52.430 --> 00:47:55.040
and implement them in a
conversation interface

00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:58.520
and that makes a whole
lot of difference.

00:47:58.520 --> 00:48:01.430
I hope I answered your
question without naming

00:48:01.430 --> 00:48:03.110
one son over the other.

00:48:03.110 --> 00:48:05.120
Who do I like and
stuff like that.

00:48:05.120 --> 00:48:09.960
ALAN HO: My favorite
is actually Audible.

00:48:09.960 --> 00:48:12.950
I like listening to
audio books and I just

00:48:12.950 --> 00:48:15.230
find it very easy
to just ask Audible

00:48:15.230 --> 00:48:18.250
to open a particular chapter and
have it start reading it to me.

00:48:18.250 --> 00:48:20.000
So that's my favorite.

00:48:20.000 --> 00:48:20.990
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:48:42.082 --> 00:48:43.290
ALAN HO: Repeat the question.

00:48:43.290 --> 00:48:44.665
AMIR SHEVAT: So
the question was,

00:48:44.665 --> 00:48:46.910
what do you do with
multi-lingual bots.

00:48:46.910 --> 00:48:49.130
And I would say,
as an engineer, I

00:48:49.130 --> 00:48:51.710
don't know what the right
answer is right now.

00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:55.010
I think this is
uncharted territory.

00:48:55.010 --> 00:48:56.852
I played a little
bit with API.AI

00:48:56.852 --> 00:48:58.310
and you can give
it different-- you

00:48:58.310 --> 00:49:00.320
can train it to give
it different languages

00:49:00.320 --> 00:49:01.850
for the same intent.

00:49:01.850 --> 00:49:03.650
And that works for some extent.

00:49:03.650 --> 00:49:07.400
But think of a place where
different people in the channel

00:49:07.400 --> 00:49:09.600
speak different languages.

00:49:09.600 --> 00:49:11.810
So I'm saying that I hear you.

00:49:11.810 --> 00:49:13.260
I don't have a good answer.

00:49:13.260 --> 00:49:16.790
But I think as an industry
we're still learning this thing.

00:49:16.790 --> 00:49:20.480
You could use API.AI to do
the trivial mapping of like,

00:49:20.480 --> 00:49:22.550
this sentence in
English, and this

00:49:22.550 --> 00:49:25.530
is how it is in
French, and in Russian.

00:49:25.530 --> 00:49:29.237
So that's a good
way of going at it.

00:49:29.237 --> 00:49:29.820
ALAN HO: Yeah.

00:49:29.820 --> 00:49:33.020
We also have a translation
service as well.

00:49:33.020 --> 00:49:35.880
There's like amazing
AI in the last year

00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:38.395
that's helping these
translations get really good.

00:49:38.395 --> 00:49:40.020
So I think it's going
to be interesting

00:49:40.020 --> 00:49:44.700
because these translation
services are, today, basically

00:49:44.700 --> 00:49:48.000
reading a lot of human
text from articles.

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:52.920
But as Google sees more and
more bots built on its platform

00:49:52.920 --> 00:49:56.221
and it's seeing a lot
of chat coming through,

00:49:56.221 --> 00:49:57.470
you're going to start seeing--

00:49:57.470 --> 00:50:01.080
I predict that these
translation services will even

00:50:01.080 --> 00:50:03.000
be very good for chat as well.

00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:04.958
So that-- we're at day zero.

00:50:04.958 --> 00:50:05.666
AMIR SHEVAT: Yep.

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:10.546
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:50:25.220 --> 00:50:27.880
ALAN HO: So the
question was, especially

00:50:27.880 --> 00:50:29.720
in a kind of a
serverless architecture,

00:50:29.720 --> 00:50:32.740
how do you go about testing--
what is the application

00:50:32.740 --> 00:50:35.270
lifecycle going to be like?

00:50:35.270 --> 00:50:37.880
Well actually, a lot of this
is-- you think about it--

00:50:37.880 --> 00:50:41.710
this is really more towards
a microservices architecture,

00:50:41.710 --> 00:50:45.040
where a lot of the concerns that
used to be bunched up together

00:50:45.040 --> 00:50:47.440
in one big monolithic
application

00:50:47.440 --> 00:50:49.300
gets broken up
into a lot of apps.

00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:51.310
So I would say the
first thing you

00:50:51.310 --> 00:50:54.040
do is having a lot of unit
tests against every single

00:50:54.040 --> 00:50:56.830
microservice, and
really figuring out

00:50:56.830 --> 00:50:59.920
what your bounded context
for each microservice

00:50:59.920 --> 00:51:01.310
actually does.

00:51:01.310 --> 00:51:02.800
So I think that's like step one.

00:51:02.800 --> 00:51:04.180
Figure out the
right architecture

00:51:04.180 --> 00:51:05.680
for your microservices.

00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:08.800
And then step two, around
your lifecycle of it,

00:51:08.800 --> 00:51:11.152
I would just treat it like any--

00:51:11.152 --> 00:51:13.660
and there's tons of
books on best practices

00:51:13.660 --> 00:51:15.490
for microservices lifecycle.

00:51:15.490 --> 00:51:17.050
I would treat it just like that.

00:51:17.050 --> 00:51:19.510
I would treat every
single one of them--

00:51:19.510 --> 00:51:21.850
you want to make sure
that every microservice

00:51:21.850 --> 00:51:25.690
that you put into production
you have a rapid process

00:51:25.690 --> 00:51:29.140
to be able to deploy new, you
have the ability to roll back,

00:51:29.140 --> 00:51:31.390
blue-green deployments,
all that good stuff

00:51:31.390 --> 00:51:34.530
that we've been
developing over the years.

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:35.955
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:51:41.064 --> 00:51:41.980
AMIR SHEVAT: Thank you

00:51:41.980 --> 00:51:42.960
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:52:04.125 --> 00:52:05.940
AMIR SHEVAT: So the
question is, how do you

00:52:05.940 --> 00:52:10.880
see the bot world evolving with
bots not being in your face,

00:52:10.880 --> 00:52:13.740
or like making too much
noise inside Slack?

00:52:13.740 --> 00:52:17.430
So I think, again, it's
a learning experience.

00:52:17.430 --> 00:52:20.160
If you think about
the first web pages,

00:52:20.160 --> 00:52:24.060
they had all these flashing
texts-- you remember?

00:52:24.060 --> 00:52:27.000
The HTML text, and
everybody use animated GIFs

00:52:27.000 --> 00:52:29.400
and flashing text in every page.

00:52:29.400 --> 00:52:33.090
I think we're a little
bit in that era.

00:52:33.090 --> 00:52:34.290
And I think we're learning.

00:52:34.290 --> 00:52:36.240
So I think in the last years--

00:52:36.240 --> 00:52:39.960
in the last year, we've worked
with a lot of bot builders

00:52:39.960 --> 00:52:44.390
to actually make the onboarding
great, doing less chat,

00:52:44.390 --> 00:52:46.440
not DMing everyone.

00:52:46.440 --> 00:52:51.180
The other thing is that you
can turn on admin approval

00:52:51.180 --> 00:52:55.650
apps, which means that only
administrators can install

00:52:55.650 --> 00:52:56.430
bots.

00:52:56.430 --> 00:52:59.040
So users can still request bots.

00:52:59.040 --> 00:53:01.830
So they could go into the app
directory and request the bot,

00:53:01.830 --> 00:53:05.530
but that goes into a queue where
the admin can approve those.

00:53:05.530 --> 00:53:11.700
So if you're seeing too many
chatter in your Slack channel,

00:53:11.700 --> 00:53:13.030
then I would turn that on.

00:53:13.030 --> 00:53:16.320
Last thing is company culture.

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:19.800
For example, in Slack, when
I talk about a certain topic

00:53:19.800 --> 00:53:21.510
off topic in the channel--

00:53:21.510 --> 00:53:24.990
if I go to the marketing channel
and start talking about code--

00:53:24.990 --> 00:53:31.980
people react with an
emoji that is a raccoon.

00:53:31.980 --> 00:53:34.200
That tells me that I'm
speaking about the wrong thing

00:53:34.200 --> 00:53:35.670
in the wrong channel.

00:53:35.670 --> 00:53:37.860
So having cultural
things like that

00:53:37.860 --> 00:53:41.220
to signal, hey, why did
you install this bot,

00:53:41.220 --> 00:53:43.397
is a good best practice.

00:53:43.397 --> 00:53:44.391
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:54:09.250 --> 00:54:10.610
AMIR SHEVAT: No, no, no, no, no.

00:54:10.610 --> 00:54:11.440
We're very--

00:54:11.440 --> 00:54:11.870
ALAN HO: Repeat the question.

00:54:11.870 --> 00:54:13.245
AMIR SHEVAT: So
the question was,

00:54:13.245 --> 00:54:15.310
how intelligent can the bot be?

00:54:15.310 --> 00:54:17.560
Can the bot have a
discussion with you

00:54:17.560 --> 00:54:21.580
about the architecture, and
that connection to design?

00:54:21.580 --> 00:54:23.980
And the answer
is, right now, no.

00:54:23.980 --> 00:54:26.950
The word intelligent in
artificial intelligence

00:54:26.950 --> 00:54:29.422
is marketing speech.

00:54:29.422 --> 00:54:31.270
[LAUGHS]

00:54:31.270 --> 00:54:37.000
It's like the AI is an amazing
tool, but bots are not sentient

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:39.550
and bots are not
intelligent in the way

00:54:39.550 --> 00:54:41.020
we perceive intelligence.

00:54:41.020 --> 00:54:42.160
They're more smart.

00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:44.590
I would call it
artificial smartness,

00:54:44.590 --> 00:54:47.230
because once you teach
something to a smart person,

00:54:47.230 --> 00:54:49.510
they know how to do it better.

00:54:49.510 --> 00:54:51.610
But there's no intelligence.

00:54:51.610 --> 00:54:54.550
At least then-- and I'm sorry--
maybe soon the overlords will

00:54:54.550 --> 00:54:56.492
take over and take
over the world

00:54:56.492 --> 00:54:57.950
and then I'll be
punished for this.

00:54:57.950 --> 00:55:01.060
But this is my opinion.

00:55:01.060 --> 00:55:05.200
What you can do is actually
facilitate workflows.

00:55:05.200 --> 00:55:08.230
And that's where the
sweet spot of bots are.

00:55:08.230 --> 00:55:11.650
So I need to approve a design.

00:55:11.650 --> 00:55:15.100
So having the post,
the design in channel,

00:55:15.100 --> 00:55:17.410
and then having a button
that says approve,

00:55:17.410 --> 00:55:22.660
instead of sending 10 emails,
that's where the bot excel.

00:55:22.660 --> 00:55:25.270
So you know the
reply all nightmare?

00:55:25.270 --> 00:55:28.240
You send an email to
a team and everybody

00:55:28.240 --> 00:55:30.070
starting replying all.

00:55:30.070 --> 00:55:31.980
You can actually
make that go away

00:55:31.980 --> 00:55:34.646
